workspace/
# ...but NOT the workspace ENTITY. 'workspace/' targets the generated runtime dir; it also matches
# src/entities/workspace/, which is real source. Those files stayed tracked in the private repo only
# because they predate this rule (gitignore does not untrack). In a FRESH repo built from a snapshot
# they were silently skipped, so the public tree never compiled. Negation, not a re-anchor: it cannot
# un-ignore anything else. (ADR-115 cutover, 2026-07-23)
!src/entities/workspace/
workspace-shared/

# Runtime app staging in the workspace volume — never tracked (Rule 0c, ADR-085). The api
# stages installed store packages into deployed-apps/ at runtime; they are a materialised
# copy of the store, not source. Left un-ignored, a stray `git add -A` commits an entire
# application into the platform trunk, which is the one thing Rule 0c exists to prevent.
# ROOT-ANCHORED deliberately: a bare `apps/` also matches docs/apps/ (45 tracked files) and
# would silently drop them from a fresh snapshot — the exact trap `workspace/` fell into above.
/apps/
/deployed-apps/
tmp/
temp/
.oshal/
_archive/
# Unmanaged local archive — retired/extension code kept on disk for reuse, never pushed.
/archive/

